 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The method adds overhead for view selection (SA-HashGS) and residual loss computation at each step. The view selection is performed periodically (every 100 epochs), not every iteration.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Standard 3DGS memory usage plus storage for the view selection state and residual loss calculations.
-*   **Performance:** SA-ResGS shows synergy with SA-HashGS, providing a reliable view sequence that enables residual supervision to further improve reconstruction in sparse or ambiguous regions [S1]. It outperforms purely dynamic selection (+ResGS) and other baselines like FisherRF, ACP, and 2D-based methods (MUSIQ, CrossScore) [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Improves reconstruction quality in sparse/ambiguous regions by combining reliable view selection with residual learning.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of the view addition schedule and residual loss weight. The specific residual loss formula is not detailed in the sources [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When reconstruction quality in sparse regions is critical and computational resources allow for the additional overhead of view selection and residual learning.
-*   **When not to use:** When real-time performance is paramount and the overhead of view selection is unacceptable.
+*   **Time Complexity:** The method adds overhead for view selection (SA-HashGS) and residual loss computation. However, by limiting the number of training views to 20, it reduces the per-iteration rendering cost compared to using all available views.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Standard 3DGS memory usage plus overhead for the SA-HashGS module and residual buffers.
+*   **Performance:** SA-ResGS shows improved reconstruction quality (PSNR, SSIM) compared to baselines like FisherRF and ACP, especially in sparse view settings [S1]. The synergy between SA-HashGS and ResGS leads to better results than using either component alone [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Stabilizes view selection, improves reconstruction in sparse/ambiguous regions, reduces computational cost by limiting views.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of the residual loss weight. Performance depends on the quality of the SA-HashGS uncertainty estimation.
+*   **When to Use:** When training data is limited (sparse views) and reconstruction quality is critical.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When computational resources are extremely limited and view selection overhead is prohibitive, or when a large number of training views are available and standard training is sufficient.
 
 ## Background
-SA-ResGS was introduced in the paper "SA-ResGS: Self-augmented Residual 3D Gaussian Splatting for Next Best View Selection" [S1]. It builds upon the principles of 3D Gaussian Splatting and active view selection, aiming to address the limitations of existing methods in handling sparse or ambiguous regions during reconstruction.
+SA-ResGS was introduced in the paper "SA-ResGS: Self-augmented Residual 3D Gaussian Splatting for Next Best View Selection" [S1]. It builds upon the 3D Gaussian Splatting framework [S1] and addresses limitations in active view selection methods. The method combines ideas from residual learning and self-augmented pre-filtering to improve robustness.
 
